  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What makes the sensitive plant fold up?",
      "a": "Touch, shaking, heat and even strong wind trigger an electrical signal in the leaf. It makes the hinge cells at the base of each leaflet lose water, so the leaflets fold.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does Mimosa close when touched?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is a pulvinus?",
      "a": "A pulvinus is a swollen joint at the base of a leaf or leaflet. Its cells act like water balloons: when they fill, the leaf opens; when water leaves, the leaf folds.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a leaf hinge work?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the sensitive plant fold its leaves?",
      "a": "Scientists think folding protects it. A suddenly drooping, shrunken leaf looks less tempting to a grazing animal, and the jerk can shake insects off.",
      "asks": [
        "What is the point of folding up?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How long does it take to open again?",
      "a": "Usually about 10 to 20 minutes, as the hinge cells slowly take water back in and swell.",
      "asks": [
        "When does the sensitive plant reopen?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Do other plants move like this?",
      "a": "Yes. Beans, prayer plants and many others fold their leaves at night using pulvini, and the Venus flytrap also uses electrical signals to move fast.",
      "asks": [
        "Why do some plants close their leaves at night?"
      ]
    }
  ],
